#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2255437b-2a7c-5d4b-ad0d-c5342e87f421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317927} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9ec6e5-63a1-5ba1-aca6-2d683cb7adfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317928} {:answer/id #uuid "b2667c7c-92a3-53f3-b37b-df98b05ec599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317929} {:answer/id #uuid "df20b430-25c3-5035-a27e-d4983f0fe92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317930} {:answer/id #uuid "1997b8a8-bf75-5ad1-9f36-d3435660bf7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2255437b-2a7c-5d4b-ad0d-c5342e87f421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317927} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1d2c490-607d-5fd2-bf7b-1eb2ec0dceb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6407551-67a9-560e-b723-72e719f9cb5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-129238" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-z-3=0$ and $x+2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c6d4629-4b4d-58b2-84f4-efc7a0475cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348755} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a591bc-546a-53a8-95a1-149c1c96f456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348756} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1d458d-ad7f-5879-9492-422252cdc8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348757} {:answer/id #uuid "49af3ebc-841a-58b3-89ed-84c600117d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348758} {:answer/id #uuid "14194db7-0721-550f-a526-89686b6a0c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49af3ebc-841a-58b3-89ed-84c600117d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348758} :answer-field/id #uuid "aecc0f8f-d563-5c1d-b722-ee5aeb098b76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32658552-b15a-5af7-93c5-13bdae918ffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-129258" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-5y+2z=0$ and $3x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b5fd7a3-28be-588a-9f00-9c215cf4ceb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348762} {:answer/id #uuid "006f54c9-8c2e-5697-88bf-61388ef3b305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348763} {:answer/id #uuid "49af2b48-ba77-5f8e-95c3-4fe6599f26f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348764} {:answer/id #uuid "56f0ef0f-2ae2-511c-94d5-9ea14596b443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348765} {:answer/id #uuid "c753c468-c883-5d88-85bb-1b118db61e40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "006f54c9-8c2e-5697-88bf-61388ef3b305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348763} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca36a7a-1ef0-5011-ad6e-eb40f3682405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "733437c7-f788-5c6d-8f50-510c19df1880" :question/math-academy-id "q-129260" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x+y-2z=0$ and $2x+y+3z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨5,-13,1⟩.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+y+3z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,1,3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i-13j+k \\\\  & =⟨5,-13,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only I and III only are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd8ed127-be85-544a-bc03-1806ffa03548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317948} {:answer/id #uuid "ada6b8f6-5526-5e24-ad84-0b05b1181052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨1,-2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317949} {:answer/id #uuid "bce644e9-3d29-5fa1-af4d-33771a072e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,-2,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317950} {:answer/id #uuid "e881c0c4-9a27-5d88-9335-7ba7bebb29d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨2,-2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317951} {:answer/id #uuid "2e11eee1-0155-5d84-96b6-03d1ea939615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd8ed127-be85-544a-bc03-1806ffa03548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317948} :answer-field/id #uuid "dddf3ab9-17c6-51d5-a9ad-06d3404c0068" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61cf4d39-9683-533e-9394-f4988b6cbe81" :question/math-academy-id "q-71446" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+3z=0$ and $3x+2y+z+8=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives    \n\n$$\nx+2(0)+3z=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=-3z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z+8 & =0 \\\\ 3(-3z)+2(0)+z+8 & =0 \\\\ -8z & =-8 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3z=-3$ and $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y+z+8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\times ⟨3,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+8j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{4}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨1,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3+1=-2,$ and $b=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308325ff-1cef-5051-a5e8-80a4f61c1912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317955} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4a1fc4-4222-51fe-b3da-0ee4c2222007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317956} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3bf136-3ab6-5529-91cb-fe72aa416075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317957} {:answer/id #uuid "05509d3f-017b-515f-81bf-22b6415a40b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317958} {:answer/id #uuid "5422e736-9203-5e36-af7d-46408e9f0d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05509d3f-017b-515f-81bf-22b6415a40b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317958} :answer-field/id #uuid "db363893-de21-563c-b1a8-bb84a9a85da9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0212fc2-f78c-514d-ac95-d9fd26dc0ceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-71463" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y-z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,3,2⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-6⟩\\ne ⟨1,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}]}
